&nbsp;
<div class="bb-quote"><b>Jay Green wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">The starter kit doesn&#39;t give you enough to go off and make your own scenarios with your own characters.&nbsp; You&#39;d have to do a lot of hacking to get those essential pieces.&nbsp; And even then a lot of stuff seems to be missing.&nbsp; When the full rules get released, a lot of these concerns may go away, but in that case you still wouldn&#39;t really want the starter set unless you want the metaplot.
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
Could this be a case of Misused Terminology? Calling it a Starter Kit could imply that it is useful for starting your own games. If they had called it a Teaser Kit or something similar, would that have assuaged some of your concerns?

[/quote]

&nbsp;

I mean, quickstarts&nbsp;Starter Kits are a thing in the RPG world, and this hits all the points.. Go and search&nbsp;in&nbsp;DTRPG, and you&#39;ll have a boatload of those.&nbsp; The SC Starter Kit is a bigger one, with a larger than average adventure, a bit of modularity in how you play, and is a way to get a taste of the system.

Same thing with Quickstarts.&nbsp; None of those&nbsp;generally give you enough to make your own scenarios.&nbsp; (Simpler systems, maybe, but Chargen is almost never included)
